WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:15.186
is no greatness where simplicity, goodness, and truth are absent," wrote the Russian writer Leo Tolstoy.

2
00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:21.168
With these few words, he expressed a belief that would inspire him for much of his life.

3
00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:28.951
Tolstoy gave the world masterpieces such as War and Peace, an epic novel about Napoleon's invasion of Russia;

4
00:00:29.572 --> 00:00:36.548
Anna Karenina, a tragic tale of forbidden love; and The Death of Ivan Ilyich, a story of spiritual

5
00:00:36.588 --> 00:00:38.310
awakening and physical death.

6
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:47.036
But despite his success, Leo Tolstoy decided in the end to leave his comfortable life behind to live

7
00:00:47.296 --> 00:00:48.397
as a simple peasant.

8
00:00:49.538 --> 00:00:59.001
Leo Tolstoy was born on August 28, 1828, at Yasnaya Polyana, his father's large country estate located 210

9
00:00:59.441 --> 00:01:01.022
kilometers south of Moscow.

10
00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:07.827
Tolstoy's mother died when he was still an infant, and he became an orphan at age nine when

11
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.088
his father died as well.

12
00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:16.193
Tolstoy and his four siblings lived with one relative after another until they settled at the home of

13
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:17.153
a distant cousin.

14
00:01:18.474 --> 00:01:25.599
After several years of studying with private tutors, Tolstoy enrolled at a university in western Russia when he

15
00:01:25.639 --> 00:01:25.859
was 16.

16
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.284
But he was more interested in going to parties, and he eventually abandoned his schoolwork in favor of

17
00:01:33.344 --> 00:01:34.505
drinking and gambling.

18
00:01:35.606 --> 00:01:43.171
In 1847, nearly penniless, 19-year-old Leo Tolstoy returned home to manage the family estate.

19
00:01:44.211 --> 00:01:50.836
Unfortunately, the family home had been sold to pay his gambling debts, and Tolstoy had to live in

20
00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:52.697
a nearby caretaker's cottage.

21
00:01:54.098 --> 00:02:01.951
On the estate, Tolstoy supervised the serfs—peasants who were forced to work for wealthy families—and decided to educate

22
00:02:01.971 --> 00:02:05.653
himself by reading classic works of literature and philosophy.

23
00:02:06.794 --> 00:02:14.819
Despite his newfound desire for wisdom and maturity, his frequent visits to Moscow offered temptations he found hard

24
00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:15.459
to resist.

25
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.282
He drank, gambled, and had many brief affairs with women.

26
00:02:20.302 --> 00:02:29.342
"Girls, silly music heat, cigarette smoke, girls, vodka, cheese, screams and shouts, girls, girls, girls!"

27
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:33.524
he noted in the diary he had started to track his self-improvement.

28
00:02:34.745 --> 00:02:41.848
Keeping a diary became a lifelong habit, and Tolstoy regularly recorded both his actions and his judgments about

29
00:02:41.888 --> 00:02:43.228
his actions in his diary.

30
00:02:44.309 --> 00:02:50.091
If he had gambled the day before, he would confess it on paper, scold himself, and then make

31
00:02:50.131 --> 00:02:51.792
a rule to correct his behavior.

32
00:02:52.884 --> 00:02:56.988
His efforts at self-discipline, however, were not very successful.

33
00:02:57.008 --> 00:03:02.092
"I am not pleased with myself," he wrote in his diary in 1851.

34
00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:10.819
Frustrated with his wasteful ways and moral lapses, Tolstoy decided to join an artillery regiment and traveled south

35
00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:12.420
to the Caucasus Mountains.

36
00:03:13.441 --> 00:03:19.466
There, he recorded keen observations of military life in his diary and began writing stories.

37
00:03:20.647 --> 00:03:28.974
In 1852, Tolstoy sent a manuscript about his early years, eventually titled Childhood, to the literary journal The

38
00:03:28.994 --> 00:03:30.794
Contemporary, which published it.

39
00:03:31.735 --> 00:03:39.377
The Contemporary also published his short stories based on his experiences in the Caucasus, most notably "The Raid."

40
00:03:40.237 --> 00:03:43.198
His work was immediately noticed and widely praised.

41
00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:49.760
"His talent seemed geniuslike, and we were a little timid before it," wrote a reviewer in 1861.

42
00:03:51.584 --> 00:03:59.188
The Crimean War began in 1853, and Tolstoy, stationed in Sevastopol, a port city on the Black Sea,

43
00:03:59.608 --> 00:04:01.830
saw firsthand the brutality of war.

44
00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:12.335
He wrote in his book, Sevastopol, in 1856, "In the blooming valley lay disfigured corpses, shoeless, in garments

45
00:04:12.375 --> 00:04:16.677
of gray or blue The odor of the dead bodies filled the air."

46
00:04:17.798 --> 00:04:24.586
Famous now, Tolstoy could have enjoyed a life of celebrity, but he discovered within himself a strict moral

47
00:04:24.646 --> 00:04:33.032
conscience. The seeming indifference of the army officers to the awful experiences of ordinary foot soldiers during the

48
00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.994
war had given him a dislike of authority.

49
00:04:37.055 --> 00:04:42.859
He could not help but compare the peasants fighting beside him to the ones on his estate, and

50
00:04:42.899 --> 00:04:48.142
he came to believe that their simple lives were the only ones with true value or meaning.

51
00:04:49.223 --> 00:04:55.015
Upon returning home, Tolstoy decided to create a school for the serfs working at Yasnaya Polyana.

52
00:04:55.936 --> 00:04:58.357
News about Tolstoy's school quickly spread.

53
00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:03.541
Some people wondered why the great Tolstoy would bother to educate peasants.

54
00:05:04.401 --> 00:05:10.786
He answered his critics by writing an article arguing that even eminent artists had much to learn from

55
00:05:10.846 --> 00:05:12.047
peasants and children.

56
00:05:13.207 --> 00:05:19.592
A restless man, Tolstoy traveled frequently, and wherever he went, he was greeted as a luminary.

57
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:26.042
But he felt uncomfortable with fame and disliked people who considered themselves better than others.

58
00:05:27.143 --> 00:05:34.526
During a trip to Moscow in 1862, Tolstoy met Sofia Behrs, the daughter of a wealthy doctor, and

59
00:05:34.546 --> 00:05:35.886
they quickly fell in love.

60
00:05:36.947 --> 00:05:42.509
On the eve of their wedding, Tolstoy gave his diaries to his new wife, saying she must learn

61
00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:43.670
everything about him.

62
00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:51.521
The Tolstoys were married for 48 years and had 13 children, 10 of whom survived infancy.

63
00:05:52.461 --> 00:06:00.083
Sofia managed the household, cared for the children, and acted as secretary to Tolstoy, spending long hours copying

64
00:06:00.163 --> 00:06:03.644
out his many scrawled manuscripts in her neat handwriting.

65
00:06:04.844 --> 00:06:14.347
Between 1865 and 1869, Tolstoy wrote War and Peace, considered one of the world's greatest works of literature.

66
00:06:15.647 --> 00:06:23.512
Set in the years 1805 to 1813, the novel tells the stories of five aristocratic Russian families during

67
00:06:23.532 --> 00:06:31.018
the Napoleonic Wars, European conflicts that were a result of Napoleon Bonaparte's desire for a French empire.

68
00:06:32.459 --> 00:06:40.525
In this book, Tolstoy explored two important themes: first, that history is created not just by great events

69
00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:49.056
or important people, but rather, by millions of ordinary people making thousands of decisions; and second, that free

70
00:06:49.116 --> 00:06:54.518
will, according to Tolstoy, is an illusion since everything is ordained by God.

71
00:06:55.818 --> 00:07:01.400
Anna Karenina, published in 1877, was regarded as another masterpiece.

72
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:08.722
Tolstoy believed that the novel, about a love affair between a married woman and a dashing military officer,

73
00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:11.023
was the best thing he had ever written.

74
00:07:11.883 --> 00:07:17.933
He had come to despise the cruelty of the wealthy and powerful, and he rejected the authority of

75
00:07:17.993 --> 00:07:21.936
both the government and society—themes he discussed in the book.

76
00:07:23.036 --> 00:07:30.120
After War and Peace and Anna Karenina were published, the world recognized Tolstoy as a literary giant, and

77
00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:31.841
he could have rested on his fame.

78
00:07:32.742 --> 00:07:37.665
But his interest lay in finding spiritual and moral truth in his own life.

79
00:07:38.705 --> 00:07:44.349
Over the years, he had come to believe that the Russian Orthodox Church was corrupt and that all

80
00:07:44.569 --> 00:07:48.092
organized Christianity misrepresented Christ's message.

81
00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:54.939
He wrote numerous essays on his spiritual beliefs, and people came from around the world to visit his

82
00:07:55.019 --> 00:07:56.700
estate and discuss his ideas.

83
00:07:57.821 --> 00:08:06.049
His wife expressed unhappiness at Yasnaya Polyana being overrun by strangers, while Tolstoy struggled daily with the desire

84
00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:09.332
to give away all his money and live his life in a simple way.

85
00:08:10.352 --> 00:08:14.936
Sofia strongly opposed this idea because she needed money to raise their children.

86
00:08:15.917 --> 00:08:19.741
Tension that would never soften arose between husband and wife.

87
00:08:19.761 --> 00:08:26.006
"We were what stood in the way of his carrying out his dream of a free, new life,"

88
00:08:26.446 --> 00:08:28.348
Sofia wrote in her autobiography.

89
00:08:29.589 --> 00:08:38.101
In November 1910, the 82-year-old Tolstoy turned his possessions and land over to his family and set out

90
00:08:38.181 --> 00:08:41.162
to finally live the life of an ordinary peasant.

91
00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:43.744
But he did not get very far.

92
00:08:44.765 --> 00:08:52.289
Within days, he fell ill, and the world-famous novelist died in a railroad stationmaster's humble shack.

93
00:08:53.570 --> 00:09:00.995
Because of his relentless search for life's meaning and his desire to shun his wealth, Tolstoy was misunderstood

94
00:09:01.075 --> 00:09:06.398
by many of his colleagues, but they could not deny that the writer had found much wisdom in

95
00:09:06.438 --> 00:09:08.700
the daily life he observed around him.

96
00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:17.085
Tolstoy's novels and short stories are regarded as among the best, most realistic descriptions of the complex lives

97
00:09:17.145 --> 00:09:19.287
of human beings to have ever been written.

98
00:09:20.327 --> 00:09:27.652
As the writer Isaac Babel said, "If the world could write by itself, it would write like Tolstoy."
